course: dictionary
lesson: 6416
title: Must: rules, needs and sure guesses
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Must is a small word with big jobs. Most of the time it means something has to happen, because it is needed or it is a rule: «He must get up by 7:00.» Breakfast and washing take 45 minutes, so getting up by 7:00 is needed.
explain: Must can also show that you are almost sure something is true. «You must be tired» means you think you are tired, based on clues. «It must have rained» means you are almost sure it rained, because the ground is wet. Use must have with the past form for something that already happened.
explain: Must not means something is not allowed. «Film stunts must never be copied» is a warning: do not copy them. When you meet must, check the words around it: is it a need, a rule, a guess, or a warning?
fact: Must is a modal verb. It has no other forms, so you never write musted or musts.
fact: Must not is the same as mustn't, and it means something is not allowed.
fact: A must is an informal noun for something you really should do or have, as in «Sunscreen is a must.»
fact: Casual speakers often say «gotta» or «have to» instead of must, as in «gotta go». Formal writing uses words like «shall» and «is required to».
exercise: «Breakfast takes 20 minutes, so he must get up by 7:00.» What does must mean here? | It is not allowed to get up | *It is needed, so he has to get up by 7:00 | He got up in the past | He is almost sure he will get up
exercise: «It must have rained.» What does must have mean here? | It will definitely rain tomorrow | It is not allowed to rain | It rained every day this week | *You are almost sure it rained, based on clues
exercise: «Film stunts must never be copied.» What does this sentence tell you? | *Copying film stunts is not allowed | Stunt performers must be paid more | Copying film stunts is easy and safe | Every film needs stunts
test: In «Sunscreen is a must», what does a must mean? | A kind of cream | *Something you really should have | A lost thing | A loud noise
test: Someone says «I gotta go.» What does gotta mean? | *A casual way of saying must or have to | A name for a petrol station | A Scottish word for don't | A kind of bag
exam: In the formal sentence «Each candidate shall provide…», which everyday word could replace shall? | Can | Gotta | *Must | Maybe
